<Project>

	<PropertyGroup>
		<ExportTriggerProject>CoverageInsight</ExportTriggerProject>
		<ExportSourceRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)</ExportSourceRoot>
		<ExportName>CoverageInsight-DEV-$([System.DateTime]::Now.ToString('yyyy-MM-dd'))</ExportName>
		<ExportDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\_export\$(ExportName)</ExportDir>

		<!-- today's date at midnight, so the zip carries no working times -->
		<ExportTimestamp>$([System.DateTime]::Now.Date.ToString('yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss'))</ExportTimestamp>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- under Export, the build does ONLY our export target (no compile, no bin/obj) -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Export'">
		<BuildDependsOn>ExportSolution</BuildDependsOn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- inline C# task: sets timestamps on all files AND folders under a root -->
	<UsingTask TaskName="SetTimestamps" TaskFactory="RoslynCodeTaskFactory"
			   AssemblyFile="$(MSBuildToolsPath)\Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Core.dll">
		<ParameterGroup>
			<Root ParameterType="System.String" Required="true" />
			<Stamp ParameterType="System.String" Required="true" />
		</ParameterGroup>
		<Task>
			<Code Type="Fragment" Language="cs">
				<![CDATA[
				var t = System.DateTime.Parse(Stamp);
				foreach (var f in System.IO.Directory.GetFiles(Root, "*", System.IO.SearchOption.AllDirectories))
				{
				    System.IO.File.SetCreationTime(f, t);
				    System.IO.File.SetLastWriteTime(f, t);
				    System.IO.File.SetLastAccessTime(f, t);
				}
				foreach (var d in System.IO.Directory.GetDirectories(Root, "*", System.IO.SearchOption.AllDirectories))
				{
				    System.IO.Directory.SetCreationTime(d, t);
				    System.IO.Directory.SetLastWriteTime(d, t);
				    System.IO.Directory.SetLastAccessTime(d, t);
				}
				System.IO.Directory.SetCreationTime(Root, t);
				System.IO.Directory.SetLastWriteTime(Root, t);
				System.IO.Directory.SetLastAccessTime(Root, t);
			]]>
			</Code>
		</Task>
	</UsingTask>

	<!--
		Runs for every project under Export, whether or not the export itself fires, so a
		build that produces no zip still says why. Silence from this target means the
		Export configuration never reached the projects at all - check Configuration
		Manager, where a project that doesn't know the configuration is mapped to Debug.
	-->
	<Target Name="ExportDiagnostics" BeforeTargets="Build" Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Export'">
		<Message Importance="high"
				 Text="Export: project '$(MSBuildProjectName)', trigger '$(ExportTriggerProject)', match=$([System.String]::Equals('$(MSBuildProjectName)', '$(ExportTriggerProject)'))" />
		<Message Importance="high" Text="Export: target folder $(ExportDir)" />
	</Target>

	<Target Name="ExportSolution"
			Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Export' and '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == '$(ExportTriggerProject)'">

		<RemoveDir Directories="$(ExportDir)" />

		<ItemGroup>
			<!-- What must not travel, named one file at a time rather than by pattern.
			     Every entry below corresponds to something this repo actually contains or
			     that a build or a run creates here. This project holds no credentials and
			     no private configuration, so unlike other projects there is nothing here
			     that is withheld for secrecy - the roster is only about noise.

			     bin, obj, .vs and .git are build and tool state. TestResults is where
			     `dotnet test` and the VS collector drop .coverage files, which are this
			     app's input rather than its source.

			     The four export patterns are the app's own output: a run produces
			     <name>-coverage.html, <name>-coverage.csv, <name>-coverage-ai.csv and
			     <name>-digest.md. They are the product of the tool, not part of it, and
			     they are large.

			     Note what is deliberately NOT excluded, since a reader may expect it:

			     Assets holds app.ico and the PNGs. They are a few hundred kilobytes and
			     add nothing to reading the code, but CoverageInsight.csproj names app.ico
			     in ApplicationIcon and app_256.png as a Resource, so a zip without them
			     does not build. A zip that cannot be built is worth less than a zip that
			     is slightly larger.

			     sample-report.coveragexml and sample-report-vs2026.xml are fixtures. They
			     are what lets somebody run the app, or reason about the parsers, without
			     a Visual Studio installation to hand. -->
			<ExportFiles Include="$(ExportSourceRoot)**\*"
						 Exclude="
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\bin\**\*;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\obj\**\*;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\.vs\**\*;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\.git\**\*;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\TestResults\**\*;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\*.user;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\*.zip;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\*-coverage.html;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\*-coverage.csv;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\*-coverage-ai.csv;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)**\*-digest.md;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)docs\**\*;
                     $(ExportSourceRoot)_export\**\*" />
		</ItemGroup>

		<Copy SourceFiles="@(ExportFiles)"
			  DestinationFiles="@(ExportFiles->'$(ExportDir)\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />

		<!-- stamp everything AFTER copy, BEFORE zip, so the zip captures the times -->
		<SetTimestamps Root="$(ExportDir)" Stamp="$(ExportTimestamp)" />

		<ZipDirectory SourceDirectory="$(ExportDir)"
					  DestinationFile="$(ExportDir).zip"
					  Overwrite="true" />

		<Message Importance="high" Text="Solution exported to $(ExportDir).zip" />
		<Message Importance="high" Text="Included: source, tests, sample reports and Assets - the zip builds as it stands." />
		<Message Importance="high" Text="Excluded: build output, .vs, .git, TestResults and any coverage reports this app has produced." />
	</Target>

</Project>
